On 7/28/07, Dora C PEARCE <dpearce at students.ballarat.edu.au> wrote:
> Dear S list,
> I have run a spatial linear regression model using a CAR covariance matrix 
> and, in addition to obtaining a significant association between the dependent 
> and independent variables, get a negative log-likelihood (= -6.176). However, 
> when I add a factor and an interaction term as additional explanatory 
> variables, the log-likelihood is positive (= 1.794).
>
> Is it OK to have a POSITIVE log-likelihood, or does this mean there is a 
> misspecification problem?

A positive log-likelihood is acceptable in a model for a continuous
response.  The likelihood in a model for such a response is the
density function evaluated at the observed data and the parameter
values.  A probability density function can be greater than one, which
implies that the log-likelihood can be positive.

